      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think you\u0027re referring to this comment, which was introduced (though later slightly modfied) in [1]:\n\n  For still (non-animated) image codecs, this will return an empty vector.\n\nLooking back, I don\u0027t think this was ever guaranteed to be true. That CL introduced animated frames in SkCodec, and SkGifCodec::onGetFrameInfo() (the only implementation at that time) returned a vector of size 1.\n\nIn its current implementation, SkCodec::getFrameInfo [2] considers an image \"animated\" if there is a single frame and onGetFrameInfo(int, FrameInfo*) returns true. So there is a bug in that the documentation doesn\u0027t match the implementation. I think we should fix one of them. I don\u0027t have a strong preference which one, but I lean towards fixing the implementation (and adding tests), since I don\u0027t see how it\u0027s useful to provide the FrameInfo for a still image. (A client can already call getFrameInfo(int, FrameInfo*) directly, if they so choose.) Neither Chromium nor Android use the version of getFrameInfo that returns a vector. (Neither does Flutter; at least not flutter/engine [3], which I think is the only piece of Flutter that uses SkCodec.) So I think it should be fine to change the implementation.\n\n[1] https://codereview.chromium.org/2045293002\n[2] https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/skia/src/codec/SkCodec.cpp;l\u003d764;drc\u003dde68be3f18ba99cc01d75903e167ca09bade253c;bpv\u003d0;bpt\u003d1\n[3] https://github.com/flutter/engine",
